---
/**
 * /contact — one address, two profiles, and what is worth writing about.
 *
 * No form, for the same reason /privacy can say the site collects nothing: a
 * form needs an endpoint, an endpoint needs a service, and a service is a third
 * party reading everything anyone types into this site. A `mailto:` needs none
 * of that, and it is the only contact affordance here.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * THE ADDRESS IS ENTITY-ENCODED, AND IT HAS TO STAY THAT WAY.
 *
 * Every character of it is a decimal character reference in the `href` below,
 * so the literal string is not in the served HTML and a scraper grepping for
 * `something@something` finds nothing on this page. The visible text is
 * "Email me"; the address is never printed as text anywhere on the site, and
 * `lib/schema.ts › contactJsonLd` deliberately emits no `email` property for
 * the same reason — the graph would hand back in plain text exactly what the
 * markup is holding back.
 *
 * Entities rather than a script that assembles the address on load, because the
 * site's own rule is that a control which cannot work is not shown, and a
 * JS-assembled `mailto:` is a dead link to anyone reading with scripting off.
 * The browser decodes character references in the HTML parser, long before the
 * URL reaches the mail handler, so this link is a completely ordinary
 * `mailto:` by the time anything acts on it. It is not encryption. It stops the
 * lazy harvesters and costs a reader nothing, which is the whole trade.
 *
 * **Do not "tidy" the href back into readable text, and do not build it from a
 * template expression** — Astro escapes the `&` in an expression result, and the
 * entities would ship to the page as literal `&amp;#97;`. It has to be static
 * attribute text.
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 *
 * Not in the nav or the section manifest on purpose: it is a footer page, and
 * it sits in the colophon row beside /about and /privacy. The screenshot
 * takedown route is not repeated here; /privacy owns it and this page points at
 * it, because two copies of a promise is one copy that can go stale.
 */
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { contactJsonLd } from "../lib/schema";

const jsonLd = contactJsonLd();
---

<Base
  title="Contact · Aayush Manchanda"
  description="How to reach Aayush Manchanda: one email address, two profiles, and an honest note about what is worth writing about and what is not."
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <header>
    <h1 class="page-title">Contact</h1>
    <p class="standfirst">
      No form, no calendar link, no chat widget. One address, two profiles, and
      an honest note about what I answer.
    </p>
  </header>

  {/*
    Outside `.doc` on purpose. `.doc a` is the prose-link treatment, bold and
    underlined, which is right for a link inside a sentence and wrong for the
    one thing this page exists to let you press. This is the index-row idiom
    instead: full-bleed, hairline top and bottom, arrow on hover.

    The href is static attribute text and every character of the address is a
    decimal character reference. See the file header before touching it.
  */}
  <a
    class="mailto press"
    href="mailto:&#97;&#97;&#121;&#117;&#115;&#104;&#109;&#97;&#110;&#99;&#104;&#97;&#110;&#100;&#97;&#50;&#64;&#103;&#109;&#97;&#105;&#108;&#46;&#99;&#111;&#109;?subject=Hello%20from%20aayushmanchanda.com&amp;body=Who%20I%20am%3A%0A%0AWhat%20this%20is%20about%3A%0A%0A"
  >
    <span class="mailto__label mono">Email me</span>
    <svg
      class="mailto__arrow"
      viewBox="0 0 16 16"
      aria-hidden="true"
      focusable="false"
    >
      <path
        d="M2.5 8h11M9 3.5 13.5 8 9 12.5"
        fill="none"
        stroke="currentColor"
        stroke-width="1.25"
      />
    </svg>
  </a>

  <div class="doc">
    <section>
      <h2 class="doc__head mono">What that link does</h2>
      <p>
        It opens your mail app with the subject filled in and a two-line
        template in the body: who you are, and what you are writing about. Fill
        those in and send it. You can delete the template, it is a prompt rather
        than a requirement.
      </p>
      <p>
        The address itself is not printed on this page as text. It is encoded in
        the link, which keeps the lazier scrapers off it and costs you nothing.
        If your browser has no mail app set up, copy the link target instead of
        clicking it.
      </p>
      <p>
        I read everything that arrives. I do not reply to everything, and I
        would rather say so here than leave you watching an inbox.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="doc__head mono">Worth writing about</h2>
      <p>
        A tool I should be running, especially if you built it. Tell me what it
        is for and what it replaces. I test things on real work rather than on a
        demo, so I will say honestly whether it fits what I do, and if it does
        not fit, that is a fast no rather than a slow silence.
      </p>
      <p>
        Something wrong on this site. A dead link, a verdict that has gone
        stale, a screenshot that stopped looking like that site a year ago.
        Corrections get the quickest reply out of me, and you do not need to be
        polite about it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Work that is actually interesting. Vetted takes a small number of
        clients, so a real problem with a real budget is a conversation worth
        having. Put the problem in the first paragraph.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="doc__head mono">Not worth writing about</h2>
      <p>
        Link exchanges, guest posts, and sponsored placements. A paid row in <a
          href="/tools">/tools</a> would break the only thing that makes /tools
        worth reading, so the answer is no before you ask it, and it is still no
        the second time.
      </p>
      <p>
        Cold pitches for services I did not ask about. Anything that opens by
        telling me it noticed my website. Requests to add a link to a page you
        have written on the same topic. None of these get a reply, and I am
        saying that here so you can spend the email somewhere better.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="doc__head mono">Elsewhere</h2>
      {/*
        Written tight against the surrounding words on purpose. A line break
        between a word and a tag is not a space to Astro, it is nothing, and the
        readable version of this block shipped as "I am@amanchanda7on X and".
        See the note in `design.md` §6.
      */}
      <p>
        I am <a class="ext" rel="me noopener" target="_blank"
          href="https://x.com/amanchanda7">@amanchanda7</a> on X and <a
          class="ext" rel="me noopener" target="_blank"
          href="https://github.com/aayushmanchanda2">aayushmanchanda2</a> on
        GitHub. Both are in the footer of every page here too. X is the faster
        route for a short question; email is better for anything that needs more
        than a sentence.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="doc__head mono">A screenshot of your site</h2>
      <p>
        If one of the screenshots in <a href="/sites">/sites</a> is yours and
        you want it gone, you do not need to email me about it. <a
          href="/privacy">/privacy</a> has the route, it is a GitHub issue, and
        you do not have to explain why. That page is also where I say what this
        site collects, which is nothing.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  /* The index-row idiom from the home page, at one row.

     `--press-scale` is dialled back for the same reason it is there: a
     full-bleed row shrinking by 4% reads as a glitch rather than a press. No
     `transition-property` on the anchor itself, because `.press` already
     declares one and a second would race it depending on stylesheet order. */
  .mailto {
    --press-scale: 0.995;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr auto;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 1rem;
    /* the gap a standfirst gets before the body of a footer page; `.doc` below
       gives its own margin back so the two do not stack into a chasm */
    margin-top: clamp(3rem, 10vh, 5rem);
    padding-block: clamp(1.1rem, 2.5vw, 1.4rem);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--hairline);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--hairline);
    color: inherit;
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .mailto:hover {
    border-color: var(--hairline-strong);
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  .mailto__label {
    /* a step up from the 11px label voice: this is the page's one action, not a
       field name beside a value */
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
  }

  .mailto__arrow {
    width: 16px;
    height: 16px;
    color: var(--faint);
    transition-property: transform, color;
    transition-duration: var(--dur);
    transition-timing-function: var(--ease-out);
  }

  .mailto:hover .mailto__arrow {
    color: var(--accent);
    transform: translateX(4px);
  }

  /* The row above already carries the distance from the masthead, so the
     document does not need the full `.doc` gap on top of it. */
  .doc {
    margin-top: clamp(2.5rem, 8vh, 4rem);
  }
</style>
